1	// "Assume no toolchain on the machine that runs it", as a check on the one
2	// mechanical half of it: a script may not probe for a Swift toolchain with
3	// `command -v swift` unless `xcode-select -p` has already gated it.
4	//
5	// The owner's Mac installs the DMG from CI and has no Xcode command line tools.
6	// `command -v swift` reports success there anyway, because `/usr/bin/swift` ships
7	// on every Mac as a *stub*: run it without a developer directory and it does not
8	// fail, it pops the "install the command line tools?" panel — an 8 GB download
9	// prompted at the person diagnosing a dead microphone. The probe is therefore not
10	// a weaker test than `xcode-select -p`, it is a test of something else entirely
11	// (the stub's presence, which is universal). `xcode-select -p` fails cleanly when
12	// no developer directory is configured, so it is the gate; `command -v swift` may
13	// only run behind it, as `mac/scripts/diagnose-mic.sh` does.
14	//
15	// Same-or-earlier, not same-line: the gate has to *precede* the probe, and an
16	// `if xcode-select -p …; then` several lines up is as good as an `&&` on the same
17	// line. Backslash continuations are joined first so a probe split across physical
18	// lines is still read as one command, and reported against its first line.
19	//
20	// `#` comments are stripped before matching, in both directions. The script this
21	// rule was learned on *documents* the trap directly above the gated call — "`command
22	// -v swift` is NOT a usable test" — and the first draft of this check flagged that
23	// sentence (the real-tree fixture is what caught it). A commented-out gate is not a
24	// gate either, so the same stripping runs before the gate is credited.
25	//
26	// Dependency-free by design (a local pack must load without the canon mount),
27	// so the finding object is built by hand rather than via the engine helper.
28	
29	// The probes that ask "is there a swift?" — `\bswift\b` deliberately does not
30	// match `swiftlint`, `swift-format` or any other tool that merely starts that way.
31	const PROBE = /\b(?:command\s+-v|which|type\s+-p|hash)\s+(?:\/usr\/bin\/)?swiftc?\b/;
